From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server: a saved registry hive is on the disk before it replaces the old one

The wineserver saves a hive by writing a temporary file and renaming it
over system.reg (or a user's hive), with no fsync. After a power loss the
rename can be on the disk and the data not: a QA machine whose host was
hard-reset came back with an 18 MB system.reg of zeros -- all of HKLM gone,
and with it the login screen (wine: "... is a 32-bit installation"). The
temporary file is now flushed and fsync'd before the rename, and the folder
after it, so a crash leaves either the old hive or the new one.
---
--- a/server/registry.c
+++ b/server/registry.c
@@ -2334,13 +2334,28 @@
     }
 
     save_all_subkeys( key, f );
-    ret = !fclose(f);
+    /* on the disk before it replaces the old file: renamed in unwritten, a
+     * power loss left a hive of zeros (a QA machine lost all of HKLM, and its
+     * login screen with it, to a hard reset) */
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_NOFSYNC
+    ret = !fflush( f ) && !fsync( fileno( f ) );
+#else
+    ret = !fflush( f );
+#endif
+    if (fclose( f )) ret = 0;
 
     if (tmp[0])
     {
         /* if successfully written, rename to final name */
         if (ret) ret = !rename( tmp, filename );
         if (!ret) unlink( tmp );
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_NOFSYNC
+        else if ((fd = open( ".", O_RDONLY | O_DIRECTORY )) != -1)
+        {
+            fsync( fd );  /* and the rename itself */
+            close( fd );
+        }
+#endif
     }
 
 done:
